# Querying & Mutating the Board

Three primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern, plus CLI for humans:

## Concrete Assignment Default

When a prompt already supplies a Task/Bug ID and task inputs, query only fields
needed for the next decision:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status checklist outcomeResources }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'
```

Do not add routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }` orientation.
Use `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)` only after an unknown
call. Board-wide observability and plans belong to an orchestrator routing
decision; full reads remain explicit audit/debug surfaces.

## DSL Queries — primary read approach for agents

Compact queries that return structured output. Zero session overhead (no MCP handshake), batch multiple queries in a single call. **This is what agents should use for all structured reads.**

**Output format:** Use `--format compact` for token-efficient output (~40% fewer tokens). Default is `--format json`.

```bash
# Board overview
task-board q --format compact 'summary()'

# Whole-project readiness with the existing summary folded in
task-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'

# Scoped readiness at each board level
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260211-def) { observability-story }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { observability-task }'

# Single element lookup
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status assignee }'

# Filtered listing with field preset
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'

# Agent monitoring
task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'

# Batch: multiple queries in one call (semicolon-separated)
task-board q --format compact 'get(T1) { status }; get(T2) { status }; get(T3) { status }'

# Zero-argument and parameterized operations can share a batch
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(); models(agent=codex)'

# Execution plan
task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'

# Schema introspection — discover available fields, presets, operations, and mutations
task-board q 'schema()'
task-board q 'schema(operation=project_config)'
task-board q 'schema(mutation=set_status)'

# Target-only spawn policy (explicit opt-in in every profile)
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

**Operations:** `get(ID)`, `list(filters...)`, `count(filters...)`, `summary()`, `plan(scope?, mode=?, active=?)`, `agents(stale=N?)`, `models(agent?)`, `project_config(view?, role?, agent?)`, `whoami()`, `schema(operation?|mutation?)`
**Field projection:** `{ id status assignee }`; base presets: `minimal`, `default`, `overview`, `full`, `progress`, `planning`; observability presets: `observability-project`, `observability-epic`, `observability-story`, `observability-task`
**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue.
**Format:** `--format compact` (agent default) or `--format json` (TUI/programmatic)

**First-time setup:** Under the full profile, `schema()` returns the complete API contract. Under lite, skip the proactive full read and request only the failed/unknown call with `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)`. Parser errors for a known call include that same typed signature directly.

### Scoped signatures

`schema(operation=NAME)` and `schema(mutation=NAME)` return one flat signature object: `kind`, `name`, a typed `signature`, description, parameters, and examples; mutations also report `destructive` and `idempotent`. They do not include unrelated fields, presets, operations, or mutation contracts. Provide exactly one scope. `schema()` without a scope retains the full legacy response.

Known-operation parser failures preserve the underlying typed parse error and append the relevant signature. This lets an agent repair a call without reading the full schema.

### `project_config()` runtime shape

`project_config()` is always resolved by the local CLI, including when normal
board reads and writes target a remote server. Its current top-level keys are
`configured_mode`, `effective_mode`, `board_source`,
`explicit_remote_override`, `project_root`, `config_path`, `config_exists`,
`local`, `remote`, `whoami`, `goals`, `spawn`, `codex`, and `version_control`.

The no-argument shape remains the role-optional general config surface in every
profile, including the full default. For a spawn decision, opt in explicitly:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

`role` is required. `agent` is optional and otherwise follows the same
preferred-provider/schedule/runtime-affinity resolver as `task-board spawn`.
The narrow result contains `enabled`, `max_parallel`, a small effective
agent-context profile/source header, the effective allowed and target providers,
the active schedule window, canonical role, merged role ceiling for the target
provider, provider selection mode, and launch-composition enabled/status. It
excludes local/remote auth, `whoami`, Codex session, nested agent-context policy,
project paths, all unrelated provider ceilings, and version-control fields.
Active schedule allow-sets and provider refusals use the same fail-closed
resolver as spawn.

The lite profile's resolved `queries.project_config=spawn-preflight` setting
guides agents toward the target-specific call; it does not rewrite a bare
`project_config()` invocation. Supplying a view or target argument opts into
the narrow projection, whose role remains mandatory.

The policy members relevant to spawning and status mutation are:

| Path | Shape |
|---|---|
| `spawn.preferred_agent` | Legacy configured provider string, or `null`. It is retained only as compatibility evidence. |
| `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` | Currently effective `{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}`, or `null`. During an active schedule window, `source` is `schedule`. |
| `spawn.schedule` | `{configured, timezone, config_key, windows, active_window, base_preferred_agentic_system}`. `timezone` is always `UTC`; every projected window also carries its normalized bounds, key, and provider-system projection. |
| `spawn.launch_composition` | `{enabled, config_key, contract, schema_version, degradation}` without invoking `agents-infra`. |
| `spawn.ceilings` | `{contract_version, configured, config_path, codex, claude, qwen}`; each provider reports `configured` and only its configured model/criterion/effort/role fields. |
| `spawn.selection` | Required flags plus provider effort vocabularies and supported model criteria. |
| `spawn.execution` / `spawn.tracking` | Executable background mode and durable run-observation contract. |
| `goals` | Static compact `board-goal-v2` capability with required Session Manager native reflection and the one-release `primary-goal-v1` / `board-goal-v1` adapters. The legacy `goals.primary` descriptor remains nested alongside the v2 fields for the compatibility release. It never embeds the current objective and is omitted from `view=spawn-preflight`. |
| `version_control` | `{configured, confirm, desired_commit_time, enforced_types, owner_message, acknowledgement_parameter, acknowledgement_value, config_key}`. |

Local mode uses `spawn.*` keys. Remote mode uses `remote.spawn.*` when that
object is present and non-null; otherwise it falls back to the whole root
`spawn` object. The reported `spawn.config_key_prefix` and nested `config_key`
values always identify the effective source. `preferred_agentic_system` is the
canonical config spelling; `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.

### Project observability preset family

`summary() { observability-project }` returns the whole-project view. The
element presets work with the existing `get` and `list` operations; the task
preset also applies to Bugs because Tasks and Bugs share the leaf estimate
contract.

Every preset exposes the same nested `observability` field:

- `scope` identifies the project or selected Epic/Story/Task/Bug.
- `summary` carries the existing `byType`, `active`, and `blocked` summary,
  scoped to that element and its descendants.
- `readiness.estimateKind` carries the leaf or calculated
  Story/Epic/project projection state without dumping every missing leaf.
- `readiness.coverage` always reports `estimated`, `unestimated`, `total`,
  `ratio`, and `complete`, including fully estimated scopes.
- `readiness.rolledEstimate` is the known Fibonacci sum even when coverage is
  partial. Query the separate `estimate` field on the incomplete Story/Epic to
  retrieve its exact `unestimatedTaskIds` drill-down list.
- `isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard dependency state across the
  scope, while `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` aggregate
  derived cross-container waiting. The nested summary retains blocker details.
- Only the project preset adds `primary_goal`. Its active form is
  `{active:true,id,revision,objective,updated_at}`; after a clear or before
  any creation it is `{active:false,latest_ref}` (where `latest_ref` can be
  `null`). Epic, Story, Task, and Bug observability deliberately do not repeat
  this board-wide parent intent or treat it as element acceptance.

Plain `summary()` keeps its legacy response shape for compatibility.

## DSL Mutations — structured writes for agents

Write operations through the same DSL engine. All mutations support `--dry-run` preview. Destructive mutations require `--confirm`.

```bash
# Status changes
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=development)'

# Evidence-backed role handoff (preferred producer end transition)
task-board handoff TASK-12 --role developer

# Task/Bug estimate enum
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=notEstimated)'
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'

# Create elements
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task)'
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task, description="Implement feature", scope="Backend only", ac="Tests pass")'

# Update README fields
task-board m 'set_details(TASK-12, title="New title", description="Updated desc")'

# Rename overview name/slug while preserving ID, README, progress, children, and resources
task-board m 'rename(TASK-12, name=better-task-name)'

# Assignment
task-board m 'assign(TASK-12, agent=claude-opus)'
task-board m 'unassign(TASK-12)'

# Dependencies
task-board m 'link(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'
task-board m 'unlink(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'

# Checklist
task-board m 'check_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'uncheck_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-12, text="Write tests")'

# Notes
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Progress update")'        # append
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Replace all", set=true)'  # replace

# Move to new parent
task-board m 'set_parent(TASK-12, parent=STORY-03)'

# Resources
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="...", description="Design doc")'
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="revised", description="Design doc v2")'
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md")'

# Destructive — preview first, then confirm
task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-12)'
task-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-12)'

# Batch mutations (semicolon-separated)
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=done); set_status(TASK-13, status=development)'
```

**Safety flags:**
- `--dry-run` — preview mutation without applying. Returns what would change
- `--confirm` — required for destructive mutations (`delete`, `delete_resource`)
- Mutation arguments are schema-checked. Unknown named arguments and extra positional fragments are rejected with typed errors before any write runs.

